# What `project/agents.py` rendered for each type before the standing briefs moved out of its string
# literals and into `assets/toolkit/agents/`, as SHA-256 over the UTF-8 bytes of each generated file.
#
# The move is only worth doing if it changed nothing a project receives, and "generate before and after"
# cannot be run across one commit. So these were captured by running the generator at fee9ce4, the commit
# this change was written on top of, and the new path has to reproduce them exactly
# (`tests/test_briefs.py`). Anyone can take the same reading: check out fee9ce4, run
#
#     PYTHONPATH=src python3 -c "import hashlib; from slipwai.layout import Layout; \
#         from slipwai.project.agents import agent_files; \
#         print('\n'.join(f'{hashlib.sha256(t.encode()).hexdigest()}  {d}  {p}' \
#             for d in ('.', 'delivery') for p, t in sorted(agent_files(Layout(d)).items())))"
#
# and compare it with the lines below.
#
# Two layouts, because the one value a brief interpolates on every type that has it is `{{make}}`, and a
# project that keeps its delivery material in a subdirectory says `make -f delivery/Makefile`. A record
# taken at the default layout alone would pass with the substitution stubbed out to the empty string.
#
# A deliberate edit to a brief changes these, which is the point: the line a reviewer has to see change is
# the one saying which file's bytes a wording change moved. Re-read them the same way after the edit, with
# `agent_files` on the edited tree, and say in the commit which type was reworded.
#
# <sha256>  <project.json layout.delivery>  <path in the generated project>
